WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.772
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:06.700
SPEAKER: BigQuery
is a fully managed

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:09.670
petabyte-scale low-cost
analysis warehouse.

00:00:09.670 --> 00:00:11.770
You can access and
share analytics insights

00:00:11.770 --> 00:00:14.380
in your organization
with a few clicks.

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:16.810
While BigQuery has robust
security and governance

00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:19.960
controls to keep your data
safe within your organization,

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:21.640
it's important to
keep sensitive data

00:00:21.640 --> 00:00:23.860
inaccessible to
unauthorized third parties

00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:26.497
and reduce data
exfiltration risks.

00:00:26.497 --> 00:00:28.330
Event Threat Detection
is a built-in service

00:00:28.330 --> 00:00:30.100
for the Security
Command Center premium

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:33.340
tier that continuously
monitors your organization

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:37.170
and identifies threats within
your system in near real time.

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:40.360
It includes a finding type for
BigQuery data exfiltration,

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:43.660
which can help alert you when
an unauthorized actor attempts

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:46.480
to extract or expose
sensitive data in BigQuery,

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:49.340
whether on purpose
or by accident.

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:52.390
BigQuery data exfiltration
works by examining audit logs

00:00:52.390 --> 00:00:53.840
for two scenarios--

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:56.170
a resource is saved
outside your organization,

00:00:56.170 --> 00:00:59.680
or a copy operation is attempted
that's blocked by VPC Service

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:02.330
Controls, or an attempt
is made to access

00:01:02.330 --> 00:01:05.019
BigQuery resources that are
protected by VPC Service

00:01:05.019 --> 00:01:06.610
Controls.

00:01:06.610 --> 00:01:09.040
In order to use BigQuery
data exfiltration,

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:12.220
first enable Event Threat
Detection with an SCC premium

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:13.450
subscription.

00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:15.250
If you want to set
up ETD, check out

00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:18.310
our episode on how to use
Event Threat Detection.

00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:20.920
Let's imagine a malicious
actor was tasked with stealing

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:26.560
the financial data set from the
organization GCP-SEC-DEMO-ORG.

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:29.380
They attempt to push results
to an external table located

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:32.290
in a different Google Cloud
organization, project,

00:01:32.290 --> 00:01:34.750
and BigQuery destination table.

00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:38.800
If the GCP-SEC-DEMO-ORG
organization did not have ETD

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:41.770
enabled, the malicious actor
can quickly perform the query

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:44.770
and extract data to another
project under a new table

00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:47.110
if they have the
right permissions.

00:01:47.110 --> 00:01:50.200
While the action is logged, it
happens mostly under the radar.

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:52.880
By the time someone notices,
the job will be complete,

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:55.240
and the data has likely
been exfiltrated.

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:58.370
Luckily, this organization
has enabled ETD.

00:01:58.370 --> 00:02:00.345
And when the malicious
actor attempts,

00:02:00.345 --> 00:02:02.590
ETD will surface the
finding immediately,

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:05.560
including when it
was discovered,

00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:08.410
the category, under
Source Properties,

00:02:08.410 --> 00:02:11.380
the project found in,
and data associated

00:02:11.380 --> 00:02:13.990
with the exfiltration attempt
including the detection

00:02:13.990 --> 00:02:15.880
category and rules--

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:20.890
exfiltration to an external
table, and jobState SUCCEEDED.

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:23.350
Below, you can find the
destination BigQuery data set

00:02:23.350 --> 00:02:27.030
and table IDs and the person
who submitted the job.

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:28.810
The finding directly
links to the job,

00:02:28.810 --> 00:02:31.300
so that if the job
had not yet succeeded,

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:35.110
you can now follow the link and
stop the job before completion.

00:02:35.110 --> 00:02:37.480
You can also add a
VPC service perimeter

00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:40.360
by heading to the VPC
Service Control Console.

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:42.820
Service perimeters are an
organization-level method

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:45.490
to protect Google Cloud
Services in your projects

00:02:45.490 --> 00:02:48.670
in order to mitigate the
risk of data exfiltration.

00:02:48.670 --> 00:02:49.780
Give it a name.

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:52.750
Leave perimeter type as
default. And select Enforced

00:02:52.750 --> 00:02:56.790
mode to prevent access
to protected services.

00:02:56.790 --> 00:03:02.940
Select the project, Restricted
Services, BigQuery API,

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:08.340
VPC accessible services,
Access Level, and click Save.

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:10.920
The idea is that the legitimate
users of the data set

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:13.290
are aware of the
perimeter violation.

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:15.870
If some malicious actor
got access to the data set

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:18.180
but was not aware of the
perimeter, attempting

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:20.160
to exfilltrate or
even legitimately use

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:22.710
BigQuery at all would
result in the violation

00:03:22.710 --> 00:03:24.960
finding being generated.

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:27.240
Now, if the malicious
actor attempts

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:29.790
to exfilltrate financial
data from BigQuery

00:03:29.790 --> 00:03:33.570
to an external table
again, they are blocked.

00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:35.700
VPC Service
Controls-- Request is

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:40.110
prohibited by organization's
policy, vpcServicecontrols.

00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:42.180
Going back to the
SCC threats page,

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:45.240
a new finding under the BigQuery
Data Exfiltration category

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:49.020
has surfaced showing the
VPC permanent violation.

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:49.860
Congratulations.

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:52.260
You've set up BigQuery
data exfiltration

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:55.230
using Event Threat Detection
for Security Command Center

00:03:55.230 --> 00:03:58.230
and learned how to respond
to a finding from it.

00:03:58.230 --> 00:04:01.280
[MUSIC PLAYING]

